    },
    {
      "text": "using a group size G = 4, a weighting factor λ = 0.02, a learning rate of 1e-6 and a batch size of",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "### Step 1: <interleave>9,16,23,30<interleave>Identify the values and ratios given. The height of the bridge (AB) is 6",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "### Step 2:<interleave>2,9,16,23,26,30,33<interleave>Assign the given ratio to the triangle‘s sides: AB corresponds to",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "### Step 3:<interleave>23,26,30,33<interleave>Calculate the length of BC. Since AB is 6 units (which corresponds to",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "3 parts), then each part is 2 units. BC, having 4 parts, will be 4 * 2 = 8 units.",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "### Step 4:Use the Pythagorean theorem, \\\\( AC = \\\\sqrt{AB^2 + BC^2} \\\\).",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "### Step 5:Substitute the values into the equation: \\\\( AC = \\\\sqrt{6^2 + 8^2} = \\\\sqrt{36 + 64} = \\\\sqrt{100} = 10",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "'z' units, where z = 6.0. If the slope of the overpass AC follows a 3:4 ratio, what is",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "### Step 1: <interleave>22,23,25,29,30,32<interleave>Identify the known segments: \\\\( MP = 11 \\\\), \\\\( PO = 27",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "### Step 2:<interleave>21,22,27,28,29,34<interleave>The hypotenuse \\\\( MO = MP + PO = 11 + 27 = 38 \\\\).",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "squared \\\\( NP^2 \\\\) is equal to the product of the segments it divides the hypotenuse into: \\\\( NP^2 = MP \\\\times",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "### Step 4:Calculate the product: \\\\( NP^2 = 11 \\\\times 27 = 297 \\\\).",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "### Step 5:Find \\\\( NP \\\\) by taking the square root: \\\\( NP = \\\\sqrt{297} \\\\).",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "### Step 1:<interleave>0,1,30,31,34<interleave>Recognize that triangle ABC is a right triangle with angle ACB",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "### Step 2:<interleave>31,34<interleave>Identify that angle CBO, as part of triangle OBC, measures 30 degrees.",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "### Step 4:<interleave>0,1,34<interleave>Calculate angle ABC. Since angle CBO is 30 degrees and angle ACB is 90",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "degrees, angle ABC = 90 - 30 = 60 degrees.",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "### Step 5:<interleave>0,1,30,31,34<interleave>Find angle A using the angle sum property of a triangle:\\nAngle A +",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "Angle ABC + Angle ACB = 180 degrees\\nAngle A + 60 + 90 = 180\\nAngle A = 180 - 150 = 30 degrees",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "that BD = 6 and AC = 8. The perimeter of diamond ABCD is ().",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "side length.\\n\\nPerimeter \\\\( = 4 \\\\times 5 = 20 \\\\)",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "The quadrilateral ABCD is a rhombus since its diagonals are perpendicular. \\n\\n[asy]\\npair A,B,C,D,O;\\n\\nA = (0,4);\\nB =",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "(6,0);\\nC = (-3*sqrt(3), 3);\\nD = (0, 4);\\nO = (0, 0);\\ndraw(A--B--C--D--cycle, black+linewidth(1));\\ndraw(A--C,",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "black+linewidth(1));\\ndraw(O--C, black+linewidth(1));\\ndraw(O--D, black+linewidth(1));\\nlabel(\"$A$\", A, N);\\nlabel(\"$B$\",",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "B, E);\\nlabel(\"$C$\", C, dir(315));\\nlabel(\"$D$\", D, N);\\nlabel(\"$O$\", O, S);\\n[/asy]\\n\\nLet\\'s label the intersection point",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "of the diagonals O. \\n\\nSince OA = OC = 4 and OB = OD = 3, we have OB = 3 and BD = 6.\\n\\nSo, the perimeter of the",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "rhombus ABCD is 4OA + 4OB = 4 * 4 + 4 * 3 = 16 + 12 = 28.\\n\\nTherefore, the answer is 28. \\n\\nSince the question asks",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "each other at right angles. Therefore, AO = OC = AC/2 = 8/2 = 4 and BO = OD",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "= BD/2 = 6/2 = 3.",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "The hypotenuse is AD, with AO = 4 and BO = 3.\\n\\n\\\\[\\nAD = \\\\sqrt{AO^2 + BO^2}",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "= \\\\sqrt{4^2 + 3^2} = \\\\sqrt{16 + 9} = \\\\sqrt{25} = 5\\n\\\\]",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "O, and AC = 6.0, then AB = ()",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "### Step 4: Apply the Pythagorean theorem in triangle ABC: \\\\( AB^2 = AC^2 + BC^2 \\\\).",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "### Step 5: Substitute the known values into the Pythagorean theorem: \\n\\\\( AB^2 = 6^2 + 8^2 \\\\).",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "### Step 6: Calculate the squares: \\n\\\\( 6^2 = 36 \\\\) and \\\\( 8^2 = 64 \\\\).",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "### Step 7: Sum the squares: \\n\\\\( AB^2 = 36 + 64 = 100 \\\\).",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "### Step 8: Take the square root to find AB: \\n\\\\( AB = \\\\sqrt{100} = 10 \\\\).",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "hypotenuse.\\n\\nGiven that AO is the radius (4.0), we use the Pythagorean theorem in triangle OAB:\\n\\nAB = √(OA² +",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "OB²) = √(4.0² + 4.0²) \\n\\nAB = √(16 + 16) \\nAB = √32 \\nAB ≈ 6.4\\n\\nSo the answer is approximate 6.4. It seems there is",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "(being the diameter) is twice the radius: BC = 8.",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "In ABCD, given that AB < BC, the perpendicular bisector of diagonal AC intersects AD at point E.",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "### Step 3: Since AB < BC, consider potential equalities or relationships with the line segment created by the",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "### Step 4: Express the perimeter of triangle CDE: CD + DE + EC. Given EC = EA due to E being on the perpendicular",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "By symmetry, BC = 50 cm, AD = 50 cm, so AE = 25 cm. From the right triangle AEC, AC^2 - AE^2 = CE^2, so AC^2 - AE^2",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "= CE^2 = AD^2 - AE^2. Therefore, CE = 25 cm. Therefore, the perimeter of triangle CDE = 75 cm. ×",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "EA = EC. The intersection at E means that E is equidistant from A",
      "section_id": "sec:appendix",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 22
    }
